    {
      "id": "NEX-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "anchor": "inputs-face-determinant-product",
      "title": "The product of local determinants in integral coordinates",
      "statement": "With P the product of local Hermitian determinants, D the absolute discriminant of L, and d=[L:ℚ]N, one has 2^d det G=D^N P². For a feasible h, 1≤D^N d^d P. A matrix A of N independent common minimal integral columns has |N_{L/ℚ}(det A)|≤D^N d^d.",
      "hypotheses": "L totally complex of positive degree, N≥1 and h a Hermitian family at one embedding per conjugate pair. The identity is algebraic for every such h. Positivity and the lower bound require q_h(y)≥1 on every nonzero integral vector. The frame consequence requires its columns to have q_h-value one.",
      "correspondence": "The archival proof gives the conjugate embedding the transposed Hermitian block, factors a block determinant using the full embedding matrix, and combines the resulting identity with the real Gram lower bound. The normalized frame estimate subsequently applies Hadamard to the pullbacks. The imaginary off-diagonal example checks the factor 2^d and the finite index convention.",
      "dependencies": [
        "NEX-01"
      ],
      "equations": [
        "inputs-face-discriminant-identity"
      ],
